From 6761a44829a0a2fb41b44b49e48d8779652fe818 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 02:36:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 216/217] hir/from_ast: exported macros turn `:pat`, `:item`
 and any `:ty` fragment back into tokens

Following 0213 (expressions, blocks, statements), the remaining fragments
a `macro_rules!` that another macro defines can hold. Patterns and items have no
re-lexable printer; the proc-macro input visitor already writes both (every
derive and attribute macro input goes through it), so it now writes to
a `TokenSink`: `ProcMacroInv` (unchanged protocol) or a `TokenCollector` that
makes mrustc tokens. `ProcMacro_PatternTokens`/`_TypeTokens`/`_ItemTokens`
expose it to HIR lowering. A top-level or-pattern is parenthesised, as the
fragment was one pattern. Types other than paths (which stay as 0076 wrote
them) go the same way: `[u32; 3]` hit "TODO: emit_type - Convert interpolated
macro fragment".

All fragments written out for one macro now share one hygiene scope (the
re-lexed ones each had their own), so `$p => $e` with `$p:pat` binding a name
that `$e:expr` uses resolves, as the fragments of one invocation do in rustc.

Needed on the way:
- `Token::clone` of an `:item` fragment was a TODO, hit when the definition of
  the inner macro is parsed from its token tree; and `AST::Item::clone` had no
  `impl`, negative impl, macro invocation or extern block (the clone functions
  existed for the last two). Modules (and macro 2.0 items) still can't be
  cloned, so a `mod` in such a fragment still hits that TODO.
- the visitor now writes static/const, type alias, macro invocation (as
  `path! { .. }`), `extern crate` and inline module items, `:meta` fragments,
  and primitive types (were TODOs); a pattern macro was written without its
  delimiters (`m ! x`), now `m!(x)`; `impl A + 'a` had a doubled `+`.
- `cfg_attr` is written as is for these tokens (the visitor evaluates it for
  proc macros, which would apply the defining crate's cfgs).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
